<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Obfuscation config for the customer-deliverable PDecision build. Renames types/members of our own three
  assemblies (PDecision, DecisionMaking, PokerUtil) to hide the strategy logic from decompilation. Everything
  else (NuGet/third-party DLLs, the PokerSuite.* packages we don't build here) ships unmodified.

  {{WORKDIR}} is a placeholder: build.ps1 generates a real Obfuscar.xml from this file by substituting it with
  an absolute path, because Obfuscar's own $(CONFIGURE_LOCALLY) variable substitution was removed (see README.md) and
  <AssemblySearchPath> specifically requires an absolute path. Do not run this file directly - run build.ps1.

  SKIP LIST — do not remove an entry without re-reading obfuscation/README.md "Why every entry here exists".
  Two failure families, both confirmed by hanging/crashing an actual obfuscated build and diagnosing it with
  dotnet-dump (see README.md for the full story):

  1. Reflection-by-member-name. Renaming breaks code that looks a member up by its ORIGINAL name:
     - PDecision.ApiSettings / HandHistorySettings / DatabaseSettings: bound from appsettings.json via
       IConfiguration.Bind(), which matches JSON keys to property names by string.
     - PDecision.HealthResponse / ErrorResponse: REST responses, pinned by [JsonPropertyName] anyway (belt
       and suspenders).
     - DecisionMaking's JSON data-transfer classes (gtoJson, SolverSolution, HandSolver, ChartHand,
       PlayerSavedStats, Answer): deserialized with Newtonsoft.Json from strategy/solver/stats files or the
       legacy TCP wire format, with no [JsonProperty] mapping, so Newtonsoft matches by property name too.
     - DecisionMaking.SettingsStrategy / PreflopSettings: Visual Studio-generated ApplicationSettingsBase
       classes. Every property getter/setter does this["<hardcoded original name>"], resolved via a
       SettingsProperty collection built by reflecting over the ORIGINAL property names. These back most of
       the strategy's tunable parameters, not just startup config.

  2. Any type with a field/property typed as one of OUR OWN enums (listed below), while the type itself gets
     renamed. This is not about JSON, [init], or reflection at all - it's a more basic metadata corruption:
     renaming CasinoSettings (while leaving its property's enum TYPE, ProfileVsHumans, un-renamed) made the
     CLR throw TypeLoadException trying to load CasinoSettings at all. Renaming PlayerAccount the same way
     didn't crash, but silently corrupted it: DeepCopy<List<PlayerAccount>>'s Newtonsoft round-trip dropped
     PlayerAccount.status/cards, so a test account came back as PlayerStatus.Bot (its default int value, 0)
     with null cards, crashing two levels further down with a NullReferenceException. Every enum declared in
     these three assemblies is skipped (renaming them has no real protection value anyway - they're short
     structural labels, not algorithms - and several ARE looked up by member name too, e.g.
     Enum.Parse<ProfileVsHumans> against the literal text in dbo.CasinoSettings.ProfileVsHumans). Every OTHER
     type with an enum-typed member is also skipped: use obfuscation/audit-enum-members.ps1 after any change
     that adds a new enum-typed field/property, to find whether its containing type needs adding here too.
-->
<Obfuscator>
  <Var name="InPath" value="{{WORKDIR}}\obf-staging" />
  <Var name="OutPath" value="{{WORKDIR}}\obf-output" />

  <Var name="KeepPublicApi" value="false" />
  <Var name="HidePrivateApi" value="true" />
  <Var name="RenameFields" value="true" />
  <Var name="RenameProperties" value="true" />
  <Var name="RenameEvents" value="true" />
  <Var name="HideStrings" value="true" />
  <Var name="OptimizeMethods" value="true" />
  <Var name="SuppressIldasm" value="true" />
  <Var name="UseUnicodeNames" value="false" />
  <Var name="MarkedOnly" value="false" />

  <AssemblySearchPath path="{{WORKDIR}}\obf-staging" />

  <Module file="{{WORKDIR}}\obf-staging\PDecision.dll">
    <SkipType name="PDecision.ApiSettings" skipMethods="true" skipFields="true" skipProperties="true" />
    <SkipType name="PDecision.HandHistorySettings" skipMethods="true" skipFields="true" skipProperties="true" />
    <SkipType name="PDecision.DatabaseSettings" skipMethods="true" skipFields="true" skipProperties="true" />
